    "ui_label": "Arakawa C-grid",
    "description": "Staggered arrangement in which the horizontal velocity components are separated from each other and from mass, each component sitting at the centre of a cell edge and directed normal (perpendicular) to that edge: mass-related quantities at the cell centre, the zonal component on the east and west edges and the meridional component on the north and south edges. Divergence and the pressure-gradient force are then differenced across a single grid spacing, giving the best representation of gravity-wave propagation and geostrophic adjustment of any of the arrangements once the Rossby radius is resolved; the cost is that the Coriolis term requires averaging neighbouring velocity points. It is the most widely used arrangement in current atmosphere and ocean models. The D grid places the same components tangential to their edges instead.",
